# Turing Completeness of Graphs and Hypergraphs

## Introduction

Turing completeness is a fundamental concept in computer science that defines the ability of a system to perform any computation that a Turing machine can, given enough time and memory. Graphs and hypergraphs, as mathematical structures, are powerful tools for modeling relationships and computations. This document explores whether graphs and hypergraphs are Turing complete, provides examples of Turing-complete graph rewriting systems, and discusses the computational capabilities of append-only graph rewriting systems.

## Graphs and Hypergraphs in Computation

### Graphs

A **graph** consists of a set of vertices (nodes) connected by edges (lines), representing pairwise relationships between entities. Graphs are widely used in computer science for modeling networks, dependencies, and various structures.

### Hypergraphs

A **hypergraph** generalizes the concept of a graph by allowing edges, called **hyperedges**, to connect any number of vertices. This feature enables hypergraphs to represent more complex relationships involving multiple entities simultaneously.

### Modeling Computation

Graphs and hypergraphs can model computational processes by representing states and transitions:

- **States as Nodes**: Each node represents a state of a part of the universe or a piece of data.
- **Transitions as Edges**: Edges or hyperedges represent transitions between states or the application of functions.

#### Assumptions

- **State Transitions**: A hyperedge connects the old states of one or more parts of the universe to the new states of one or more parts of the universe.
- **Algorithm Representation**: At least one of the states is the algorithm to be executed.
- **Input Parameters**: Zero or more of the states are input parameters to the algorithm.
- **External Side Effects**: External side effects are represented by states of the universe that are not connected to any other hyperedges.
- **Transition Function**: The transition function executes the algorithm, creating new states.
- **Architectural Models**: This model can represent a pipes-and-filters architecture, a dataflow architecture, and an infinite state machine.

## Turing Completeness of Graph Rewriting Systems

### Graph Rewriting Systems

A **graph rewriting system** consists of a set of rules for transforming graphs. These rules define how a subgraph can be replaced with another subgraph, enabling the simulation of computational processes.

### Turing-Complete Graph Rewriting Systems

Graph rewriting systems can be Turing complete if they can simulate any computation that a Turing machine can perform. Examples include:

#### Double-Pushout (DPO) Approach

The DPO approach provides a formal framework for graph transformation based on category theory. It allows the definition of precise and general graph rewriting rules.

- **Reference**: Ehrig, H., & Kreowski, H.-J. (1976). *Parallel and Sequential Graph Grammars*. [Link](https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0025723)

#### Interaction Nets

Interaction nets are a paradigm where computations are modeled through the interaction of agents represented as nodes in a graph. Rewriting rules are local and can be applied in parallel, making them suitable for efficient computation.

- **Reference**: Lafont, Y. (1990). *Interaction Nets*. In *Proceedings of the 17th ACM SIGPLAN-SIGACT Symposium on Principles of Programming Languages* (pp. 95–108). [Link](https://doi.org/10.1145/96709.96718)

#### Graph Reduction in Functional Programming

Graph reduction represents expressions as graphs and reduces them according to rewriting rules. It is a key implementation technique for non-strict functional programming languages like Haskell.

- **Reference**: Peyton Jones, S. L. (1987). *The Implementation of Functional Programming Languages*. [Link](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-implementation-of-functional-programming-languages/)

### Hypergraph Rewriting Systems

Hypergraph rewriting extends graph rewriting by allowing transformation rules to be applied to hypergraphs, accommodating more complex structures.

#### Turing-Complete Hypergraph Rewriting

An example is the use of hypergraphs to represent and implement the lambda calculus, which is Turing complete.

- **Reference**: Fernández, M., & Mackie, I. (1997). *A Calculus for Interaction Nets*. [Link](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3975(97)00207-7)

## Append-Only Graph Rewriting Systems

### Characteristics

In append-only graph rewriting systems, transformations are restricted to adding new nodes or edges without modifying or deleting existing ones.

### Computational Limitations

Append-only systems are generally **not Turing complete**. The inability to modify or delete portions of the graph prevents the implementation of certain computational constructs, such as loops and conditional branches that require state changes.

### Workarounds

While limited, append-only systems can encode state changes within the growing structure of the graph. However, this approach often leads to impractical complexity and is insufficient for achieving full Turing completeness.

- **Reference**: Ben-Amram, A. M., & Pollack, S. (2011). *What Can We Compute Without Deletion? On the Computational Power of Random-Access Machines with Append*. *Information and Computation*, 209(8), 1065–1080. [Link](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ic.2011.05.001)

## Applications of the Model

### Pipes-and-Filters Architecture

The model can represent a **pipes-and-filters architecture**, where data flows through a sequence of processing components (filters) connected by channels (pipes).

### Dataflow Architecture

It is applicable to **dataflow architectures**, where the execution of operations is driven by the availability of input data rather than a predefined control flow.

### Infinite State Machines

By dynamically representing states and transitions, the model can simulate an **infinite state machine**, accommodating systems with unbounded or evolving state spaces.

## Conclusion

Graphs and hypergraphs, when utilized within comprehensive rewriting systems, can achieve Turing completeness, enabling them to perform any computation that a Turing machine can. However, append-only graph rewriting systems lack this capability due to their inability to modify existing structures, which is essential for general-purpose computation. Understanding these distinctions is crucial for the development of computational models and architectures that leverage the strengths of graph-based representations.

